No terminal needed — it's invoked from inside a Claude Code conversation. Four ways, in the order you'll actually want them:

1. @-mention — guaranteed, and the one you want


@agent-chainsmoke-reviewer review the tree before I start Phase 10
Type @ and pick it from the typeahead, or type @agent-chainsmoke-reviewer manually. This is the only form that guarantees your agent runs rather than leaving it to my judgment.

2. Just ask — relies on delegation


Have the chainsmoke-reviewer subagent do a Phase 10 check-up
This works, and your description field is what I match against to decide. Yours is written well for this: it names the trigger cases (review, phase check-up, status read, second opinion), so plain "do a phase check-up" should route to it without you naming it at all.

3. Terminal — whole session runs as the reviewer


claude --agent chainsmoke-reviewer
Different thing, worth knowing: this doesn't delegate to the agent, it makes the entire session adopt its system prompt, tools, and model. Useful if you want a dedicated review session with no other work mixed in. You can also pin it per-project with {"agent": "chainsmoke-reviewer"} in .claude/settings.json, though I wouldn't — you'd get the reviewer for every task in this repo.

4. Follow-ups — once it's run, "continue that review and now look at pkg/net" resumes the same agent with its context intact, rather than starting a cold one.

One thing /agents won't do: as of v2.1.198 it no longer opens a creation wizard, it just points you at .claude/agents/. So editing the file directly, the way you did, is now the normal path — not a workaround.

